From 4e61b982640e22ff9af58000c92fcdcd81be89e6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gavin John <gavinnjohn@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 15:55:54 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 84/93] input: resume a repeat where the content ends, not
 where the dates do

A demuxer dates the blocks it hands over but does not always measure them, so
es_out takes the length of the last block of a pass from the step before it.
That is right only when the item ends on a whole step. The 4 s transport stream
here is 174 aac frames: its last PES carries nine of them where the step before
it was fourteen, and the five frames of difference - 116 ms - is time the pass
was credited with and has no content for.

A repeat in place starts the next pass where the last one stopped, so every
seam opened a 116 ms hole in what reached the audio output. The output was
holding more than that, so it never ran dry and never played the hole: it
simply played the new pass 116 ms before the dates said, and read that back as
drift. The correction can pull 9 cents, which is 21 ms over a 4 s pass, so it
saturated and stayed there, detuning for the whole pass; the residue carried
into the next seam, and when the two together passed the 120 ms at which the
output stops treating an offset as drift it dumped a fifth of a second of
silence instead. Three runs of six passes, on the dummy output with a 50 ms
device:

    before   3 saturations and 2 silences per run, holes of 116118, 116003,
             116117, 116026, 116117 us at the five seams
    after    no saturation, no silence, and no hole over 1 ms at any seam

The lengths are not unknowable, only unknown to the demuxer: a block leaving a
packetizer carries the length of the frame it holds, and what leaves a decoder
is samples. So let the decoders say how far what they were given reaches, and
place the repeat by that, keeping the demuxer's estimate only where there is no
decoder to ask. The last frame of a pass needs prising out first - a packetizer
cannot call a frame complete while more data might still belong to it, and at
the end of the data it sits on it until the next pass arrives, which is too
late to be measured. An end of stream would drain it, but a drain also empties
and stops the output that is covering the loop, which is what the repeat path
exists to avoid; so ask for the packetizer alone, leaving the output running.

Measured the same way on the other containers to hand, a 4 s item looped three
times: mpeg-ps, wav, ogg, matroska, mp3, avi and au are unchanged, aiff goes
from 50 ms of hole at each seam to none, m4a from 6 ms of overlap to none, and
flac plays 32 ms more of itself per loop while its own seam trouble - 64 ms
that never reaches the output - stays as it was. opus is unchanged too and
still loses 16 ms a loop somewhere else.

